      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Talpa was first called Rio Chiquito and settled in the early 1700s as ranchos near Taos began to grow.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talpa%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The settlement sits on the old Spanish land grant of Don Cristobal de la Sena.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talpa%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Around 1820, a private chapel was built for the Duran family along the Rio Chiquito.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talpa%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Juan Pedro Cruz, born about 1855, was a famed weaver who supplied sarapes and rugs to Taos Pueblo.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
